      <h1>Ghost Audio</h1>
      <p class="lede">An investigation into transcript text deletions</p>

      <div class="callout">
<p><strong>Bottom Line, Up Front:</strong> This page deals with speech that failed to become text. That is a training-data quality problem.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How big of a problem is this?</strong></p>
<p><strong>A:</strong> Potentially significant. LLMs were built largely from text available on the Internet. Future training datasets will increasingly depend on converting audio and video into text. If speech is silently omitted during transcription, the resulting dataset can preserve a conversation that never actually happened.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Why does this happen?</strong></p>
<p><strong>A:</strong> There is not one failure mode. In Episode 136, one missing utterance was recoverable but survived only under certain decoder trajectories and implementations. Another was so weak that no tested decoder configuration recovered it naturally. Source audio, segmentation, beam search, timestamp behavior, and decoder implementation can all matter.</p>
<p>Higher-quality audio does not necessarily produce a more complete transcript.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How do you find missing text when there is no record that it ever existed?</strong></p>
<p><strong>A:</strong> There is no panacea.</p>
<p>A few useful signals emerged:</p>
<ul>
<li>Compare multiple plausible decoder configurations and review regions where they disagree.</li>
<li>Treat localized repetition or hallucinated speech as a possible indicator that the decoder is struggling with real audio underneath it.</li>
<li>Inspect decoder probabilities when possible to distinguish speech that is recoverable from speech that remains below the model's natural emission threshold.</li>
<li>Listen to the source audio. Some omissions leave no textual fingerprint at all.</li>
</ul>
      </div>

