<section>
<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>Late in development, I switched from YouTube audio rips to episodes downloaded directly from Libsyn.</p>
<p>The Libsyn files were higher quality, consistently named, and gave me one canonical source per episode instead of a collection of full episodes, clips, shorts, and mirrored uploads spread across YouTube.</p>
<p>Libsyn was intended as a going-forward source change. I ran several older episodes through the pipeline as a spot check.</p>
<p>That is when Episode 136 produced something I did not expect.</p>
<p>Two versions of the same episode produced different transcripts.</p>
<p>Both had gone through substantially the same downstream processing. But a statement by David Friedberg appeared in an older transcript generated from the lower-quality source and disappeared from the newer Libsyn transcription.</p>
<blockquote><p>David Friedberg: “Oh, my god.”</p></blockquote>
<p>The omission itself is tiny.</p>
<p>What bothered me was what it implied.</p>
<p>I had spent an enormous amount of time building QA around transcript errors after transcription: bad speaker attribution, fragmented sentences, contaminated turns, duplicated text, broken alignment.</p>
<p>I had not seriously considered that the transcriber itself might silently delete speech upstream of all of that work.</p>
<p>There was no blank line.</p>
<p>No <code>[inaudible]</code>.</p>
<p>No broken timestamp.</p>
<p>No indication that anything was missing.</p>
<p>I spent the next several weeks hunting for speech I knew existed, like one of those 1990s paranormal ghost-hunting shows.</p>
<p>Hence the name:</p>
<p><strong>Ghost Audio.</strong></p>
<p>Ghost Audio is a deletion in word error rate: speech exists in the source audio but never appears in the resulting transcript.</p>
<p>WER normally assumes a reference transcript exists.</p>
<p>Episode 136 had no trusted reference.</p>
<p>Producing one was the entire point of this project.</p>
<p>And the transcript I was producing was already demonstrating that it could not safely be treated as ground truth.</p>
<p>The important issue is therefore not whether one short phrase disappeared.</p>
<p>It is <strong>training-data completeness</strong>.</p>
<p>A transcript with silent gaps can:</p>
<ul>
<li>remove the cause of a later response,</li>
<li>merge two ideas that were never adjacent,</li>
<li>distort speaker relationships,</li>
<li>erase disagreement or acknowledgment,</li>
<li>and train a model on conversational transitions that never occurred.</li>
</ul>
<p>The dangerous part is that the transcript can still read perfectly well.</p>
</section>
