<section>
<h2>Triage: Ruling Out Audio Failure</h2>
<p>Before opening the decoder, I wanted to answer a much simpler question:</p>
<p><strong>Is the missing speech actually represented in the audio?</strong></p>
<p>That sounds silly because I could hear it.</p>
<p>But I wanted something better than “I can hear a person talking.”</p>
<p>I worked down the acoustic stack.</p>
<h3>VAD</h3>
<p>I varied onset and offset thresholds to test whether speech segmentation itself was dropping the utterances before the decoder had a chance to see them.</p>
<p>Both Pyannote and Silero detected speech activity around the relevant regions.</p>
<p>Segmentation alone did not explain the disappearance.</p>
<h3>Energy</h3>
<p>RMS energy remained above the local noise floor around both ghost regions.</p>
<p>Neither interval was simply digital silence.</p>
<h3>Pitch</h3>
<p>I used a Chamath-only interval immediately before Friedberg's line as a subtraction reference.</p>
<p>After spectral subtraction, pYIN recovered an F0 trace overlapping Friedberg's “Oh, my god” that was not visible as clearly in the raw mixture.</p>
<p>That is suggestive rather than conclusive: spectral subtraction can introduce artifacts.</p>
<p>Gerstner's region was different.</p>
<p>I did not recover a convincing pitch contour there.</p>
