<section>
<h2>Timestamp and Segmentation Behavior</h2>
<p>The next clue came from timestamp generation.</p>
<p>Whisper timestamp tokens are easy to think of as metadata added after the words have been chosen.</p>
<p>They are not.</p>
<p>They are part of the generated token sequence itself.</p>
<p>Changing timestamp behavior changed whether Friedberg survived decoding.</p>
<p>In one controlled reference-Whisper/PyTorch test on a default 0–30 second chunk, disabling timestamp generation with <code>without_timestamps=True</code> recovered his missing line.</p>
<p>Other controlled WhisperX experiments showed different behavior: changing timestamp-token settings could also cause Friedberg to appear or disappear.</p>
<p>At first this looked like a simple configuration fix.</p>
<p>It was not.</p>
<p>The important observation was broader:</p>
<p><strong>timestamp generation changes the sequence-generation path.</strong></p>
<p>A token-by-token PyTorch decode made that visible.</p>
